(window.LADDER_CARDS["44·crypt"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["44·crypt"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"44·crypt.1.1","q":"What are the points of y² = x³ + 2x + 2 over ℤ/17? What does the square-count technique say, and why does x = 5 give two points?","a":"The pairs (x, y) of the clock that make it true, and one more, the far point O; for each x the curve has two points if x³ + 2x + 2 is a square other than 0 on the clock, one if it is 0, and none if it is not a square; at x = 5, 125 + 10 + 2 = 137 = 1, a square other than 0, the square of 1 and of 16: (5, 1) and (5, 16)","w":["All 17 · 17 pairs; each x gives exactly one point; x = 5 gives two because 5 is prime","The pairs with y = 0; each x gives two points always; x = 5 gives (5, 5) and (5, 12)","The whole-number answers below 17; each x gives a point when x is a square; x = 5 gives two because 137 is prime"],"why":"Nine values of x give a square: 9 · 2 + 1 = 19 points, with O."},
 {"u":"44·crypt.2.1","q":"How is P + Q found from the line through P and Q? What does the chord-add technique give for 2P with P = (5, 1)?","a":"The line put into the curve gives a cubic in x with zeros at P’s x and Q’s x, so a third zero R, and P + Q is R flipped, (x_R, −y_R); with slope λ the sum has x = λ² − x_P − x_Q and y = λ(x_P − x) − y_P; for 2P the tangent slope is (3 · 25 + 2)/(2 · 1) = 77 · 2⁻¹ = 9 · 9 = 13 on the clock of 17, so x = 169 − 10 = 6 and y = 13(5 − 6) − 1 = 3: 2P = (6, 3)","w":["P + Q adds the two points place by place; 2P = (10, 2)","P + Q is the third point R itself; 2P = (6, 14)","P + Q is the middle of the chord; 2P = (5, 2)"],"why":"The group has 19 members, a prime, so every point but O has order 19."},
 {"u":"44·crypt.3.1","q":"What does the shared-multiple technique say, and which part is a belief? How do the phone and the earbuds both reach (6, 3) with a = 3 and b = 7?","a":"If one device keeps a count a and sends aP, and the other keeps b and sends bP, then a(bP) = b(aP) = abP, which both can find; that a listener who knows P, aP and bP cannot find it is a belief: no fast way is known to find a from P and aP; the phone sends 3P = (10, 6), the earbuds 7P = (0, 6), and each multiplies what it received by its own count: 21P = 2P = (6, 3), since 19P = O","w":["Both send their counts; nothing is a belief; they reach (6, 3) by adding (10, 6) and (0, 6)","Each keeps aP and sends a; the regrouping is the belief; they reach 10P","a(bP) = (a + b)P; it is proved safe; they reach (6, 3) because 3 + 7 = 10"],"why":"The curve’s keys are short: 256 bits against about 3000 on a prime clock."},
 {"u":"44·crypt.4.1","q":"How does a device in the middle read a conversation agreed by the shared-multiple technique? What does the six-digit technique say, and why must each side commit to its random number first?","a":"It catches each side’s public point and sends its own in its place, so each side shares a secret with it, and it passes everything on, reading it; if both devices show six digits made from both public points and a fresh random number each committed to before seeing the other’s, a device in the middle makes the two screens agree with chance one in a million; having to commit before it learns what the other will send, it cannot steer the two numbers to agree","w":["It finds a from aP; the six digits are the secret itself; committing first keeps the digits short","It jams the channel; the six digits agree always; committing first is for speed","It listens on all channels; the six digits fail one time in six; committing lets the middle device choose both"],"why":"The check is a person reading two screens."},
 {"u":"44·crypt.5.1","q":"What does the hop-share technique say? Why does a jammer on one channel cost only about 20 slots a second, and why is the hopping order not the secret?","a":"A receiver or a jammer fixed on one channel meets, on average, one slot in 79 of a pair hopping over 79 channels evenly; of 1600 slots a second, 1600/79 ≈ 20 are lost, about 1.3%, and a lost slot is sent again; a listener with a wideband radio hears all 79 channels at once, so the order hides nothing: the key from the shared-multiple technique is the secret","w":["A jammer on one channel stops the link; it costs 1600 slots a second; the hopping order is the secret key","A jammer meets one slot in two; it costs 20 slots because the pair stops hopping; the order is secret because it is random","A jammer meets no slot; nothing is lost; the hopping order is secret because only the pair has the clock"],"why":"Hopping spreads the talk so no one narrow source of noise can stop it."},
 {"u":"44·crypt.6.1","q":"Why does adding A and B not give the secret? Why is hopping not what keeps the talk secret?","a":"A + B = 10P, not 21P: the secret needs ab, not a + b; and a wideband listener hears every channel: the key makes the talk secret","w":["Adding A and B does give it; and hopping is the secret","Because points cannot be added; and hopping is too slow","Because A + B is O; and the channels are too wide"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
/* end phase F und_r44 */
