(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["2·crypt"]=[{"q":"A term z is hidden in the group {a, b, c}. What does a listener who hears the group hold?","a":"z = a ∨ z = b ∨ z = c","w":["z = a ∧ z = b ∧ z = c","z = a","nothing at all"],"why":"In over a listed set is an or across the list (2k1)."},
 {"q":"A term is hidden in a group of one. How many members does the listener have to try?","a":"one: the group gives the term away","w":["none: a group of one is empty","two","it cannot be said"],"why":"A set with one member has that member and no other (2e5)."},
 {"q":"A listener steals the condition and never hears the group. What does the listener hold?","a":"a sift with no set to sift from: it picks out nothing","w":["the term","half of the term","the group"],"why":"A sift needs a set to sift from (2f13)."},
 {"q":"A condition is so of the term and of three other members of the group. Is it a key?","a":"no: a key has to fail for every other member","w":["yes: it is so of the term","yes: any condition is a key","no: a key must be so of every member"],"why":"A key leaves exactly one member."},
 {"q":"The term is not in the group. What does the right key sift out?","a":"not the term: whatever comes out of a sift was in the group","w":["the term, if the key is right","the whole group","the term and one other member"],"why":"Sifting never adds (2f8)."},
 {"q":"A listener has two clues, φ and ψ. Which members are left?","a":"the common members of the two sifts: sifting by φ ∧ ψ","w":["the members of either sift","the sift by ψ only, the later clue","the members that fail both"],"why":"Sifting by one condition and then another is sifting by their and (2j4, 2g8)."},
 {"q":"Three conditions give every member of a group of eight its own row. How many members does each clue keep, of those still left?","a":"half","w":["one","all but one","a third"],"why":"Eight, then four, then two, then one."},
 {"q":"A secret is a part of a group of five. How many leaves does its keep-or-drop tree have?","a":"32","w":["10","25","5"],"why":"One keep or drop for each member, and each doubles the leaves (2h7)."},
 {"q":"A group gains one member. What happens to the tree of all its parts?","a":"every leaf splits in two","w":["it gains one leaf","it gains two leaves","nothing: the parts are the same"],"why":"Each old part comes with the new member kept and with it dropped (2h7)."},
 {"q":"Four sets are drawn overlapping inside a border. How many pieces can they cut it into?","a":"16","w":["4","8","12"],"why":"Each piece is a row, and each set doubles the rows (2j1)."},
 {"q":"Sets d, b, c inside a border. Which pieces does (d ∖ b) ∩ c shade?","a":"TFT only","w":["TFT and TFF","TTT and TFT","FFT only"],"why":"In d, not in b, in c."},
 {"q":"A gate's rule is (d ∩ b) ∖ c. A claim of every group reads TTT. Does the gate open?","a":"no: the rule refuses a member of c","w":["yes: more memberships never hurt","yes: TTT is in every set","it cannot be said"],"why":"A rule with a ∖ refuses a claim too many."},
 {"q":"“May open” holds of (a, k), (b, k) and (b, m), and of nothing else. Which holder can open every thing kept?","a":"b","w":["a","both","neither"],"why":"b has an arrow to k and an arrow to m."},
 {"q":"“Can pass for” holds of (e, a), and “may open” of (a, k). Nothing says e may open k. Can e reach k?","a":"yes: by the two arrows, followed head to tail","w":["no: no arrow runs from e to k","only if a agrees","no: the two arrows are of different kinds"],"why":"A route is arrows followed head to tail."},
 {"q":"A clue settles one keep or drop of a tree with 64 leaves. How many leaves are still standing?","a":"32","w":["63","62","16"],"why":"One decision settled cuts the tree in half."},
 {"q":"A gate’s rule is x ∈ d. The people who ever come are a, b and c, and only b is in d. Which set does the gate let in?","a":"{b}","w":["d","{a, b, c}","∅"],"why":"The people who come, sifted by the rule: {a, b, c} ∩ d."},
 {"q":"A rule is said aloud, and its keeper knows that no one who comes is in the group it names. Whom does it let in, and what does a use of it show the keeper?","a":"nobody; and a use shows someone claiming a membership that no true comer holds","w":["nobody; and a use shows the keeper nothing","everyone in the group, wherever they are; and a use shows nothing","the keeper alone; and a use shows who the keeper is"],"why":"The people who come, sifted by the rule, are the empty set (2f10). A rule kept for that is a decoy."},
 {"u":"2·crypt.1.1","q":"A term is hidden in a group. What does a listener who hears the group hold?","a":"the group, and not which member: an or across it","w":["the term","nothing","the key"],"why":"Each member is one more option, and the listener cannot tell the options apart."},
 {"u":"2·crypt.2.1","q":"Why is a key a sift?","a":"the key is a condition kept private, and sifting the group by it leaves the term alone","w":["the key is the term itself","the key is the group, said aloud","the key is the first member of the group"],"why":"The group is one half of the secret and the condition is the other (2f5)."},
 {"u":"2·crypt.3.1","q":"Why do a listener's clues combine the way sifts combine?","a":"each clue is a condition, and sifting by one and then another is sifting by their and","w":["each clue names a member, and names add up","each clue is a group, and groups merge","clues do not combine: the last one stands alone"],"why":"The common members of the sifts are left (2j4)."},
 {"u":"2·crypt.4.1","q":"A secret is a part of a group, and not a member. In what set is it hidden?","a":"the set of all the parts of the group, which doubles with each member the group gains","w":["the group itself","the set whose only member is the group","the empty set"],"why":"𝒫 of the group (2h1, 2h7)."},
 {"u":"2·crypt.5.1","q":"An expression in several sets is given. Why is checking one piece short, and finding a shaded piece long?","a":"checking reads one row; finding, with nothing else to go on, goes through the rows, and they double with every set","w":["checking and finding both read every row","finding reads one row, and checking reads them all","a long expression has a long check"],"why":"Show an example is cheap for the one who holds the example (2c7)."},
 {"u":"2·crypt.6.1","q":"Why does keeping someone out take more than letting someone in?","a":"one route that says yes lets a person in; a refusal needs every route to say no","w":["a refusal needs one route shut","letting in needs every route open","the two take the same: one change to one group"],"why":"Not some is every not (2k8)."},
 {"u":"2·crypt.7.1","q":"A rule names a kind, and one member of the kind ever comes to the gate. Whom does the rule let in?","a":"that one member: the people who come, sifted by the rule","w":["every member of the kind, wherever they are","nobody, since no person was named","everyone who comes"],"why":"The rule is said aloud and the set it is read on is kept. The sift of the one by the other has one member."},
 {"u":"2·crypt.8.1","q":"On an arrow chart of “may open”, what shows that every thing kept can be opened by someone?","a":"every thing kept has an arrow coming in","w":["one dot sends an arrow to every thing kept","every dot sends an arrow out","no two arrows cross"],"why":"The other line, “someone can open every thing kept”, asks for one dot with an arrow to all of them (2c13)."},
 {"u":"2·crypt.9.1","q":"A gate's rule is built from ∩ and ∪ alone. Why does a listener who can pass for a member of any group need no search?","a":"if a piece is shaded then so is every piece with more T's, so claiming every group opens the gate","w":["such a rule shades every piece","such a rule shades no piece","the gate does not read claims"],"why":"With a ∖ in the rule, a claim too many is refused, and the listener has to choose."},
 {"u":"2·crypt.10.1","q":"Why is a problem too long to search not yet a safe lock?","a":"a listener has other ways: clues that halve what is left, and rewrites that show the piece","w":["a long search always ends early","a listener cannot search at all","no problem is too long to search"],"why":"A long search protects only against a listener who has nothing better than searching."},
 {"u":"2·crypt.11.1","q":"Why is “Checking a piece took two looks, so finding the piece takes two looks” a move to watch out for?","a":"checking reads one row; finding goes through the rows, and they double with every set","w":["finding always takes fewer looks than checking","a piece cannot be checked","checking takes as many looks as there are pieces"],"why":"Outside the collect."}];
